A||||12||fyerstarter||Bad Gas Mileage, I have searched the archivs, please help||||1||
Z||000000||fyerstarter||06-03-2001||04:59 PM||||I recently bought a new (well new to me) 87 GT auto. It runs pretty well and only has 70k orig miles on it and 10k on a rebuilt motor. My problem is that I get pretty bad gas mileage, I am comparing it to my other 87 GT stick which was running like total crap. <P>I have ran out of gas with the new car twice since I've had it in 3 weeks. I get probably 150 miles to a tank and that's on the freeway. With my other GT I can make a 200 mile trip, and then still drive around locally all weekend before having to fill it up. <P>Allright about the car. <P>-It has a new fuel filter on there (the fuel filter clogged on me so I replaced it)<P>-It has a brand new ignition module (I put new one in there just because I know how much they fail and now I have the old one as a spare  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>-Fuel pressure at the schrader tests good range with key in on position and at idle<P>-The air filter is clean<P>-The tires are properly inflated<P>-I have gotten a code twice since I owned it which was for EGR (32 I believe) but it just went away and never came back. Maybe I should replace the EGR valve and solenoid?<P>-There are no vacuum leaks that I can tell (The car idles at 900-1000 rms as soon as it reached operating temp). It does idle high at about 1500 for a few minutes when I first start it.<P>-There was an exhaust leak at the manifold which I fixed (the two bolts from one whole port were gona), I haven't been able to check the front manifold yet for leaks.<P>-I don't know how good the O2 sensor is but the car passed smog in California just 4 months ago.<P>Over all the car seems to run really good but it seems to be lacking in torque. If I don't get a huge run at it I can only do 60 up a hill, the top end speed is great though and it pulls from 60-100 like nothing. The powerband on this car is completely diff from my 87 GT 5 speed and I don't know if it's just because of the different gearing of the automatic? But this thing is a total dog off the line. Also when it is cold and I step on the gas slow and even the rpms will go up to about 1500 and then it will almost die! even though I am steadily giving it gas, this will last for about a second or two and then it will suddenly surge back up. The same thing will happpen when it shifts, it will fall flat on it's face and then jump back up. As soon as the car reaches normal operating temp the problem goes away.<P>Sorry for the long post, just trying to give as much info as possible. I searched on the archive on bad gas mileage and found lots of posts of people talking about it but nothing ever got fixed. <P>Does anyone have any ideas where I should start and what I should look at first? <P>Thanks a million guys<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||06-03-2001||05:04 PM||||First thing is check base timing. Someone around here can help you with details on that if need be...<P>Next check all the sensor connectors are good and clean. Make sure MAP sensor vac line is good and not plugged with crud.<P>make sure all vac lines go to correct ports. wrong connections can be problems.<P>EGR error isn't a good sign. You need to look at that mess more even if it hasn't come back.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-03-2001||05:38 PM||||150 miles on a "full tank" is about right for an auto trans. You did not state you "gas Milage" so one can only go by miles between fillups. You car should get 18 city and 25 or so highway MPG.||||reg||5
Z||000003||fyerstarter||06-03-2001||05:51 PM||||Well I am only comparing it to my other GT. Would the difference between a manual and auto be that big? <P>Thanks for the tips Ogre, I don't know how to check the timing but I'm gonna look at the other stuff you suggested.<p>[This message has been edited by fyerstarter (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fyerstarter||06-03-2001||07:40 PM||||Ok I replaced the MAP sensor and no change. Checked the vacuum line and it wasn't clogged with junk. There was a little bit of black gooey oily crud in the port where it plugs into the intake manifold but it wasn't clogged. <P>I noticed that while the car is cold the idle tend to jump around a bit independant of how much gas you give it. If I romp on it of course it goes up but if I give it slow steady gas the RPMS will go up, then die, then up again, and when I let off they will drop kinda slowly and then pop up again. It idles between 1200 and 1500 when cold. As soon as I revved it  a couple times and let it run for 5 mins the problem went away and idle was all good. <P>Could it be the TPS?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||06-03-2001||10:06 PM||||Sounds like an EGR in it's death-throes. Mine is doing EXACLTY the same thing. I'm happy that the EGR is a cheap part! Just don't wreak that tube for jebus' sake||||reg||7
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-04-2001||09:47 AM||||You can check the TPS easily with a voltmeter - check from the wiper pin to ground and as you open and close the throttle plate the voltage should change smoothly - the votlages are on the online manual web page:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/osg.html</A> <P>check the vacuum to the map sensor - you said its not clogged (it sucks air if you pull it off the map sensor - right?) With the engine off pull the same line off the intake plenium and it should hold a vacuum if you suck on the end - one end goes to the map and the other goes to the fuel pressure valve, under the center of the plenium where you cant see it without a dental mirror.<P>   The stumbling you are getting when you step on the gas sounds like the map sensor is not seeing the change in vacuum - so check this out thoroughly.<P>  You can block the EGR at the plenium without too much effort to see if that fixes your problem. loosen the two bolts and slide a piece of tin under the tube, tighten it back down. Also plug the vacuum line at the egr muffin with a golf tee.<P>  also, are you clearing the codes? Some error codes throw the car into limp home mode and it stays there till they are cleared. I dont think an EGR error will do that, maybe. But once you clear the codes the car will run poorly for a good 30 or 40 miles, and then will act funny once in a while for the next couple hundred while the ECM keeps fine tuning itself.<P>   I have an '85 V6 auto - I was getting 15mpg when my map sensor vacuum line was half severed at the fuel pressure valve connection. With it fixed i now get 20mpg or better, depending on how nice I drive.||||reg||1
Z||000007||loune||06-05-2001||12:07 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by fyerstarter:I also have a 87 GT auto. 150 miles per tank is not normal, even for just city driving. I average 200 per tank in city driving and 240 with some highway driving. My GT is also pretty much a dog off the line but really kicks in after 50mph. Have you pulled your plugs to see if your running too rich or lean? I would suggest replacing tho o2 sensor just to eliminate that as a possible source.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fyerstarter||06-06-2001||12:05 AM||||Update: Well the other day started it up and drove a few blocks and all of a sudden temp gague jumped into the red in a matter of seconds. Pulled over and checked the coolant and the overflow was full of oil. So looks like a headgasket or a cracked head  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Don't know if this was related or or just happened out of the blue. All I know is that this motor was rebuilt about 15k miles ago (not by me but the previous owner, I have the reciepts) and I sure as hell hope they would have put in new gaskets as part of the procedure :\ Anyway I have no garage and I live in apartments so looks like gotta take it to a shop. Estimate from the local garage is about $800 - $900 <P>Thanks for the help guys. But I think when I get this thing fixed I'm done with Fieros. I've never had nearly this many problems with any of the cars I've owned.||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||06-06-2001||08:30 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by loune:<BR><B>[QUOTE]Originally posted by fyerstarter:I also have a 87 GT auto. 150 miles per tank is not normal.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry I based my estimates on a smaller fuel tank. since 87 & 88's have 2+ gallons more fuel, 150 miles a tank is not good.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000010||fyerstarter||06-06-2001||11:38 PM||||Well actually that explains it then!  When I bought the car I was told it was an 87, and it looks just like an 87 fastback and all. But looking through the paperwork the other day I found that it is only an 86!<P>Headgasket is still toast :P But looks like the fuel prob is solved. Thanks guys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||06-07-2001||12:10 AM||||Headgaskets are a breeze. I'v done those on all my cars, and could now probally do it to the Fiero with a blindfold. Too bad you live so far away though. Im actually missing working on my car all the time now, and need someone else's to work on! ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||09:22 AM||||maybe not a light breeze. dont you have to take the exhaust manifolds off to pull the heads on a V6? Good likelyhood of snapped off manifold bolts? easyout time?<P>  if the exhaust manifolds are held on with studs you can chisle the nuts offs - much easier.<P>  can you pull the foreward head with the engine still in the car on the V6? or do you have to drop the motor? On the 2.5 this might be a weekend driveway job, but on the 2.8?||||reg||1
